illowmere village, packing a bag in a great hurry. 'Leaving us?' she asked pleasantly, and the silent partner's smile did not reach the eyes. Every answer was smooth, and every smooth answer was a lie.\n\nBut Harriet Wren shook her head. Something was missing — a motive with a human heart in it. a silver locket was not mere property; it was a promise, or a debt, or a love. She would find which before she accused anyone."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Storm Breaks', 'text': "the silent partner appeared then, wild-eyed, and confessed before Harriet Wren could speak: it had begun as a kindness, grown into a theft, and ended in a lie that poisoned Willowmere village. 'I never meant —' began the silent partner, and could not finish.\n\nHarriet Wren listened to the end, rain running off her cape. Justice, she said quietly, was not the same as punishment; but the truth belonged to everyone in Willowmere village, and it would be told.\n\nThey went out in rain capes, lanterns swinging, and found the disturbed earth — and beneath it, not treasure, but proof: letters, a photograph, and the mate to a silver locket. The whole sad story lay in the mud."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "a silver locket was restored to its rightful keeper, who wept to hold it. Harriet Wren declined all thanks, as was her custom, and slipped away before the speeches. The case was closed; the village could breathe.\n\nthe silent partner's motive, when all heard it, drew more pity than anger — a lost inheritance, a sick child, a desperate choice. Willowmere village was stern, but Willowmere village was also kind; and kindness found a way to help where the law could only punish.\n\nOn market day Harriet Wren laid it all before Willowmere village: a silver locket, the letters, the photograph, the confession. The crowd in the square was silent as a church. the silent partner stood with head bowed and did not deny a word."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Quiet After', 'text': "A week later Willowmere village